/* Both authors describe the same physical events. Neither is the narrator of truth. */
static const char *const ht_evidence_prose[HT_LEVELS*3] = {
    "Her hand, stitched inside the scarf: \"The mill belts couple neighbouring shafts. The cams must meet their worn slots. I have left the route where you can reach it. Do not mistake a path I used for a path I chose.\"\n\nOn the bark, the keeper has cut a date earlier than the day she left home. Beneath it: \"She knew which tree. I had not shown her the register.\"\n\nThe thread is hers. I recognise the uneven hem and the little backward stitch she made when tired. But the grooves beneath it are older than either message. She could have been following instructions, leaving instructions, or marking the place where one became the other. I copy both dates. Neither tells me who first brought her here.",
    "Keeper's hand: \"Do not remove the tooth belts. Each clutch turns its shaft and the next one. Record every admission before opening the crossing. Six names were entered beneath hers. She supplied the names without asking the people to speak.\"\n\nHer hand, across the word admission: \"They were too cold to answer you. You knew that. I wrote for them.\"\n\nThe column heading has been scraped away. All seven names have a square beside them, but no legend explains whether a filled square means safe, confined or missing. His dates are meticulous; her corrections are impatient. A later line in his hand says, \"I kept the last square open.\" Was he waiting for someone to arrive, or refusing to certify that they had been released?",
    "A small cam is hidden inside the wooden bell. Turning it sideways fits it into a worn notch. The mill frame carries three such slots, facing different ways. A paper loop carries two explanations of the lantern scratched on its back.\n\nHer hand: \"Three short flashes: follow. Two short and one long: someone is watching. You remember. I taught them so we could speak without words.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"She gave me both patterns before I asked. She said a warning is useful only if someone else knows it.\"\n\nI remember learning those signals alone with her. The toy makes that memory less private. Perhaps she needed an ally. Perhaps someone made her surrender the one language she could use to ask me for help. The nail tied to the toy rings each wooden bell with the same small sound.",
    "Her hand on a classroom map: \"The round feed goes to the lower contact; the square feed returns above it. I have put the ladder where you will need it. When the stair door shuts, do not wait beneath it.\"\n\nThe date precedes the recorded closure. The keeper has circled both: \"Who told her which door would lock? I had not yet received the order.\"\n\nShe has answered beneath his circle: \"There was no other way.\"\n\nThe ladder is where the map places it. The door is locked. Those facts support both accounts. She may have foreseen a danger, been warned by whoever ordered it, or chosen the closure herself. The charcoal route touches every roof where a signal can be seen. It also passes none of the doors through which someone could leave unseen.",
    "Keeper's hand on the fuse label: \"Keep the heater on its own return. Crossing that wire would short it. I disconnected the beacon after the first report. No further travellers should be called until we know where they go.\"\n\nHer hand: \"You disconnected the only light they could see. They disappeared from your windows because they went below them. That is not the same as vanishing.\"\n\nA fresh wire is taped beside the burnt one. Her repair worked; I can test that. His warning about the heater return is also correct. Neither useful instruction proves its author's account of the people below. The report itself is missing. If it came before the disconnection, his act was a response. If the date was copied wrongly, it could have caused the danger he describes.",
    "Both hands draw the same circuit: the outside feeds exchange places, while the heater keeps its return. Each contact bank can bypass, cross the upper pair, or cross the lower pair. Below that agreement, the log divides.\n\nHer hand: \"Nobody should keep the whole watch alone. I left the calls written out so another person could take over.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"These are not seven separate signatures. Look at the break in the downward stroke. The copies were made below. Ask who attended, not whose name appears.\"\n\nTwo entries contain the same tiny ink blot. I once used that blot to recognise her impatient pen. A copied duty sheet would be ordinary enough. A copied testimony would not. The watch dates run on after the map says she left the city. Either others kept her words in use, or the words were being used to keep somebody there.",
    "Her hand around the lantern handle: \"Divide the full supply equally between the two watches. Leave none in the measure. I have put my allowance with the children's. Let them think there is enough.\"\n\nKeeper's hand on the ration ledger: \"The allotment was not hers to give. She was told what would happen if the lamps went out. She agreed before I finished reading the conditions.\"\n\nHer allowance is crossed out and three smaller allowances are increased. A gift and a confiscation would leave the same arithmetic. There is no signature beneath the conditions, only a pressure mark from another sheet. I can make out the word continue. Did she consent because she understood, because she was deceived, or because the others would pay if she refused?",
    "Her hand behind the stove plate: \"A transfer stops when a tank is full or the other is empty. The taps have no smaller measure. Open the flue. The knocking frightened them, so I tied the loose pipe. Let them sleep.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"I checked the binding. It held. The knocks continued: two short, one long. She said the metal was cooling. Then she asked me to leave the room.\"\n\nThere is soot over both notes, so they were here before the last fire. The pipe can carry a signal from another room, but it can also make sounds as it cools. She may have hidden a warning in a harmless explanation. She may have recognised a warning from someone else. Or she may have been keeping the keeper from hearing the instructions she was receiving.",
    "Her hand on the rolled list: \"The supply tank holds eight measures; the smaller vessels hold five and three. Seven travelling now. Leave an eighth line. I cannot promise they will come, but I can leave room.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"The eighth was entered on the requisition before she came to this station. Someone has already counted the person she says she is waiting for.\"\n\nThe can contains both lists. On one, the eighth line is blank. On the other, it carries the first letter of my name. She could have requested extra fuel in hope. Someone else could have used that hope to bring her west. It is harder to dismiss the possibility that she knew I would follow. Every place she left for me might also have been a place assigned to me.",
    "Her hand on a ticket marked HOME: \"The wagon fouls the points. Hold its brake before using the shunt winch. The cutting has fallen. They told us the next train would not stop. We were not permitted to wait.\"\n\nKeeper's hand in the cancellation book: \"She asked me to cancel it before the snow. She said the passengers would stay if they believed a train was coming. I asked how she knew the cutting would fall.\"\n\nThe cancellation bears no signature. Its time is earlier than the collapse, but later than a weather warning pinned beside it. She could have been avoiding a foreseeable danger or manufacturing the only route the others would accept. The ticket has been punched without being used. I do not know whether that means its holder was refused travel or officially recorded as gone.",
    "Only the diverging track reaches the marsh. The points are locked by the wagon standing over them. A braked winch can pull it clear before the track is changed. Both accounts agree on that practical fact.\n\nHer hand: \"He showed us a way across. I could not have brought them through without him.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"She corrected my map. The old service spur is gone, exactly as she said. She told the others I had found the route. Why give me credit for knowledge I did not have?\"\n\nThe corrected line matches the track in front of me. Perhaps someone had supplied her a newer survey. Perhaps she gave him credit to make the group trust him. Perhaps she needed him to believe he was helping while she led him somewhere of her choosing. His next question is pressed into the paper but erased: \"Who has been here before?\"",
    "The letter says THANK YOU LAMP KEEPER in large uneven capitals. Beneath it, her familiar hand supplies the route: \"Release the brake only when the wagon is clear and the diverging route is set. Tunnel fallen. Leave this here for the next watch.\"\n\nThe keeper has added on the envelope: \"She told the child what to write. The child asked whether the lamp keeper was the one who had shut the door. She said there could be more than one.\"\n\nDictated thanks need not be false. A child may need help addressing a letter. But the name belongs to a role, not a person, and I have been treating every occurrence as a witness to the same man's kindness or cruelty. The two adults use it without explaining whom they mean. The child at least thought to ask.",
    "Her hand tied to the token: \"The entry gate belongs to the low water. Water pressure holds a gate shut when the levels differ. I gave him what we had. He said it was enough for all of us.\"\n\nKeeper's hand in the ferry book: \"No fare was taken. She presented a transfer permit. The boatman recognised the seal and opened the holding gate.\"\n\nThe seal is a boat inside three grooves. The same grooves appear on the gate lock. A permit could be authority she obtained to help the group, an order directing where they must go, or an instrument she had arranged before the journey began. The metal is old, its string new. Whoever issued it expected it to be passed from hand to hand, and did not think the people holding it needed to know its history.",
    "The operating note is in her hand: \"Close the entry behind the ferry. The sluice cannot fill an open chamber. The far bank stands two watermarks higher. Wait until everyone can stand.\"\n\nThe keeper's account says the group was held for two days under an instruction he could not countermand. He writes: \"She said the delay was hers. I saw the rope tied on their side of the water.\"\n\nA rope tied on their side could mean they controlled the crossing. It could mean they were made responsible for restraining themselves. The oldest passenger's walking stick has worn a groove into the floor. Both illness and detention would explain why he stayed in one place. Neither explains why she insisted the keeper record that she had chosen to wait.",
    "The watermarks on the chamber wall rise to the far bank. The exit gate swings only when the water meets it. Beneath it are two lists of the same seven names, one for each bank.\n\nHer hand: \"Released at dawn. All accounted for. I waited until he could stand.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"Received at dawn. All accounted for. She made me copy her count before I could speak to any of them.\"\n\nThe two phrases occupy corresponding boxes on forms with their headings washed away. Release and receipt might describe an ordinary evacuation. They might describe the transfer of prisoners. Her concern for the old man's strength can be real in either case. I lay the sheets together against the light. Their missing headings are exactly the information that would let either account become more than a persuasive description.",
    "Her hand inside the pouch: \"Six equal loads belong to the hoist, shared between its arms and brake. Keep these little stones out of the gears. We played at moving a family across the quarry while the real lift was repaired.\"\n\nKeeper's hand on the repair report: \"They knew which stones to move when she rang the bell. She called it a game. I could not get them to try another order.\"\n\nOne stone has a face which smiles or frowns depending on how it is turned. The instructions may have kept children safely occupied near dangerous machinery. They may also have rehearsed something she needed them to do without hesitation. It is possible to be gentle and still direct another person's every move. The stones do not tell me whether the children were laughing.",
    "Her scarf binds the repaired brake handle. Her note says: \"The brake takes the loads left over after the arms balance. It must carry some weight before crossing. I could not let them fall.\"\n\nThe keeper has written a load calculation beside it: \"She knew the setting before we weighed the cage. She called the margin unsafe, then changed it herself. I did not see a table in her hands.\"\n\nThe binding makes the handle safe to grip. That kindness is visible, whatever brought her here. The calculation is accurate too. She may have been trained, quietly instructed, or better at this machinery than anyone was willing to admit. Beneath the paper I find a rectangular clean patch, the right shape for a plate someone removed. The knowledge could have come from there. It could have been taken away to make hers seem impossible.",
    "Her hand: \"The left arm reaches three paces from its pivot; the right reaches two. A longer arm needs less weight. Three children, two adults, one old man. Six are still with me. Leave it ready for whoever follows.\"\n\nThe keeper has corrected the count: \"Seven, including you.\"\n\nOnce I would have read that as kindness toward someone who forgot to count herself. Now I notice that his seven matches the ferry's admission list. He may be reminding her she matters. He may be reminding her that she is subject to the same restriction as the others.\n\nOn the torn edge her next sentence begins, \"When I am no longer with them...\" The rest is gone. It could be a plan to leave, a fear of being taken away, or an instruction for what the group must do without its leader. I cannot restore the missing words by wanting one version.",
    "Her hand on the packet: \"Follow the sun pipe toward the low receiver. The dead stem still points toward the inlet. Keep enough seed dry to begin again.\"\n\nKeeper's hand on the glasshouse inventory: \"They had food, heat and beds. The exit was locked from inside. She kept the key and told them the cold would kill anyone who left.\"\n\nThe temperatures in his own ledger support her warning. A locked door could have kept the wind out, kept frightened children safe, or prevented adults from choosing a risk she would not permit. The packet is empty because the seeds were planted or because they were taken away. Someone folded it carefully enough to preserve the printed promise of a harvest. That promise is not proof that they were free.",
    "Her hand: \"A silver face reflects the beam around a corner. The black face stops it; an empty frame lets it pass. Turn the trays when they lean. Write tomorrow's count even if I am gone. Someone must know what is still growing.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"Tomorrow's count was entered before dawn. It repeats yesterday's crossed-out digit, including the correction. Ask who held the pen.\"\n\nThe label contains two identical corrections. Someone could have copied yesterday's form to save time. Someone could have used a mechanical copying arm like the one described on an old maintenance tag. The plants are real and have been tended; that does not prove the person whose writing records them was present. I have been using handwriting as a witness. Here it begins to behave like another object that can be moved.",
    "The lantern is larger than the woman carrying it. Her hand adds: \"The low receiver opens the inside latch when the beam reaches it from above. They promised they would keep it lit. I did not ask them to promise.\"\n\nThe keeper has placed an older training card beside the drawing. Its figures stand in the same order. \"The picture came before the promise,\" he writes. \"She gave them this to copy.\"\n\nChildren copy things they love as readily as things they are told to learn. The card could have helped them draw a real event. Yet the woman on it has the same shortened scarf as my sister, although its print date is older than the cuts I found in the forest. A familiar uniform? A coincidence? A picture she chose to resemble? I copy the date without choosing for the child.",
    "Her hand: \"The turbine stalls below the second scratch on its gauge. I restored power for the signal. It held through the night. You cannot ask people to wait in darkness.\"\n\nKeeper's hand on the circuit diagram: \"The recorder shares the feed. She knew that. Restoring the lamp also restored the writing arm in the tower.\"\n\nThe drawing shows one wire dividing into two. This is the first physical connection between the lights and the repeated handwriting. It proves that the systems can work together, not why she powered them. She may have accepted a dangerous side effect to call for help. She may have been told the second wire was harmless. Or the writing arm may have been the part she intended to wake. I can follow the copper. I cannot follow her intention along it.",
    "Her hand: \"The garden return must never run dry. Do not trade their heat for my call. If someone demands more for the ridge, show them this.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"She controlled the common supply. The people below could refuse her instructions only by refusing the heat that kept them alive.\"\n\nThe pipe really returns toward the gardens. Her limit really preserves a share for them. Those facts do not settle whether she was protecting people from an authority above her or making herself the authority they depended on. His account leaves out the night he requested the extra power himself; his signature remains on the request. I do not know whether that omission hides a mistake, a threat, or a part of the arrangement he cannot bear to name.",
    "Her hand: \"The ridge requires half the six-unit supply. Every transfer goes to the next branch; the return closes the loop. Leave my words for the next watch. I cannot be in every place that needs an answer.\"\n\nKeeper's hand in the recorder manual: \"A master plate can move the pen after its author has gone. The sealed original supplies the strokes; the live feed selects the lines. A signature proves a pattern, not a person.\"\n\nA diagram shows two interlocked releases. Removing power opens the master compartment and locks the live selector. Completing the feed exposes the writing arm and shutters the master. Neither action can show both surfaces. Her note may describe ordinary instructions copied for successive watches. His warning may describe impersonation. The same machine could do either. Its lock makes the difference harder, not easier, to inspect.",
    "Her hand in the remaining scarf: \"Start reading the shelter posts at the scarf. Their notches record heights, not directions. I did not know which signs would survive. I left more than one way to find me.\"\n\nKeeper's hand on a tracing: \"Her stitch marks appear on the tower plan. The plan is dated before the first lock was fitted. I thought I was following an order from elsewhere.\"\n\nThe tracing could have been made later and given an earlier date. The keeper may be looking for a way to blame her for work he performed. Yet the little backward stitch appears at a point where no traveller would need a marker, beside the master compartment. Her private habit has become part of a technical drawing. I need the original, not another person's account of it.",
    "Her hand under the stone: \"Four shelter posts trace the ascent. Match each post height to a chime, reading from the cloth toward the ridge. There was no other way.\"\n\nThe keeper's note is the least orderly of his records: \"I believed she was repeating what she had been told. Then she corrected the order before I opened the envelope. I no longer know who gave it.\"\n\nNeither page explains the correction. Someone could have warned her. Someone could have rehearsed it with her. She could have written the order herself. I think of the same phrase in the city, where it sounded like an apology. Here it could be a confession, a defence, or the statement of someone who could see a necessity the rest of us could not. The stone shelters all three readings equally.",
    "Her hand on the dark side: \"The last post is the same height as the first. Look beyond the notch. Do not answer your own reflection.\"\n\nKeeper's hand on the polished side: \"The ridge returns a test pulse. Three flashes alone cannot establish who is at the far end. She knew the distinction. She taught me to check the direction mark.\"\n\nThe marks in the circuit drawing distinguish a returned pulse from an outgoing call. I can now test something both accounts have used as proof. If the light answers automatically, it does not mean she is dead or absent. It means the answer cannot tell me that she is present. I have carried certainty uphill that neither the lamp nor the paper actually gave me.",
    "Her hand on the lamp rail: \"Use the FIRST lamp, the one on the LEFT. Leave the other watches undisturbed. I have left you a way to answer.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"Check the arrows before you call the windows witnesses. The returns are marked inward. Nobody looking out is needed to make them shine.\"\n\nThe direction marks match the diagram at the dam. Some lights that seemed to accompany me are outputs of the same circuit I have been repairing. That much is now observable. It does not tell me whether she made a guide that would survive her absence or whether someone used her guide to bring me here. The dark windows beyond those lamps remain windows. There may still be people behind them.",
    "Her hand: \"Three short flashes from the first lamp. Release between each. At the glass, you will know me by the correction. You have seen me make it a hundred times.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"Corrections are copied too. Break the circuit to expose the original. Complete it to expose the live pen. The interlock closes the other face. Decide what you need to witness.\"\n\nThe service stamp gives a fixed order: master plate sealed on day one; holding locks fitted on day four. The keeper admits fitting those locks. She refers to them as something done to her. The original might show whether her instructions predated them. The pen might show whether her instructions require her at all. The machine offers a test, not an assurance that I will like its answer.",
    "A rubbing of the cabinet face carries two messages. Her hand: \"Finish what I could not. There is a place for you here. You know which errors I make when I need you to notice.\"\n\nKeeper's hand: \"Do not confuse her words with her will. I locked the doors. I thought I was stopping the system. She said there was no other way. I do not know whose sentence that was.\"\n\nThe glass covers a sheet and a motionless pen. A broken feed releases the original instructions and shutters the pen. A completed feed powers the pen and seals the original away. The choice is physical, not a vote about which person I like. I can leave and reread my journal before acting. When I return, I must decide whether freeing her means interrupting her work or allowing it to finish.",
};

static const char *const ht_story_prose[HT_LEVELS][3] = {
    { "Three flashes crossed the fog last night. My sister taught me that signal.\n\nI have brought a book to record what I find. I want to distinguish what I see from what I hope it means.", "Her cloth marks a path through the trees. Another hand has marked it too.\n\nThe second hand calls its writer the keeper. His marks follow the same route, but his warnings face the people walking toward her.", "Some cuts in the bark are older than her trail. Did she find this way or was she sent?\n\nThe path existed before she used it. That gives her account a history without telling me whose history she entered." },
    { "Her arrows cross the roofs above the fog. The keeper has crossed some of them out.\n\nI can follow either hand far enough to find a useful instruction. Being right about a ladder does not make someone right about a person.", "A ladder is lowered beside a locked door. Escape route or the only route allowed?\n\nThe arrangement is deliberate. It offers a way onward while removing another choice. I cannot see which purpose came first.", "The lamp flashes with nobody beside it. A signal can continue after its maker leaves.\n\nUntil now I have imagined a hand behind every light. The timer carries a message without carrying proof of who is present." },
    { "The pumps are still. the service lamps burn. Someone decided what to keep alive.\n\nFuel makes every instruction costly. A request to move onward can be an order when warmth depends on obeying it.", "Seven allowances are marked on a fuel can. One has been divided among the others.\n\nThe arithmetic is visible. Whether the missing share was offered or taken is not. Both accounts know the same numbers.", "A radio clicks without forming a word. The same rhythm comes from under the floor.\n\nI recognise enough to listen again. Familiarity makes a sound feel personal before I know who, or what, produced it." },
    { "The tracks lead toward three dark cuttings. Only one signal can still open a way.\n\nThe other routes may be ruined, prohibited, or both. A safe instruction can come from someone who helped make the alternatives unsafe.", "A cancelled ticket is pinned inside a carriage. The cancellation came before the collapse.\n\nA prediction and a plan can share a date. I need to know what information existed then, not merely what happened later.", "The bridge route has been corrected by hand. Both writers credit the other for finding it.\n\nGratitude would explain that. So would a wish to move responsibility elsewhere. I keep the two statements together." },
    { "A gate and a ferry token bear the same mark. Payment and permission look alike here.\n\nThe token proves access. It does not tell me whether its holder was travelling freely or being transferred.", "The rope is tied on this side of the water. The log records two days without a crossing.\n\nSomeone could have untied it. That is not proof that they were allowed to. Nor is waiting proof that they wished to leave.", "The same names appear on both banks. One list says released. the other received.\n\nThe crossing happened. The words describe its meaning differently, and the headings that might settle it have washed away." },
    { "The hoist has been repaired with her cloth. The repair makes the handle safe to hold.\n\nThat small kindness survives either account. It cannot tell me whether she chose the work or was forced to make it possible.", "Small footprints pass between the machines. The path has been carefully kept to one side.\n\nSomeone understood the danger. Care can guide people safely through a place; it can also make them trust a guide they cannot question.", "Her note counts six. his correction says seven. He has counted her among them.\n\nPerhaps he wanted her to value her own life. Perhaps he was reminding her that her name remained on the same admission list." },
    { "New rows grow under a broken glass roof. The exit has a lock on the inside.\n\nShelter and confinement need many of the same things: warmth, supplies, walls. Only the freedom to leave distinguishes them.", "A garden count repeats an earlier correction. Even the crossed-out stroke is the same.\n\nCopying a form is ordinary. Copying the sign of a living hesitation feels different. I have been trusting the latter more than the words.", "A child drew her lantern beside an old card. The figures stand in the same order.\n\nA picture can record what happened or teach people what to expect. I do not know which image the child saw first." },
    { "A generator hums behind the spillway wall. Two wires leave the same switch.\n\nOne runs toward the lights. The other disappears into the tower circuit. Restoring one may have meant accepting the other.", "The return pipe carries water back to the beds. The person at this valve controls their heat.\n\nProtecting that supply can be kindness or power over the people who need it. An instruction is harder to refuse when cold follows refusal.", "The diagram links the lamp to a writing arm. A master plate can move a pen without its author.\n\nThe machine offers a physical explanation for the repeated strokes. That establishes an ability, not which pages were made with it." },
    { "Her stitch mark appears on a tower plan. The plan is dated before the first lock.\n\nThe keeper may be shifting blame; the date may be copied incorrectly. The original would tell me more than this tracing.", "She wrote there was no other way. The keeper says she corrected his sealed order.\n\nCoercion, warning and foresight remain possible. Her certainty is evidence that she knew something, not proof of how she knew it.", "Three flashes answer from the ridge. The diagram also shows an automatic return.\n\nA reply can feel like recognition even when no person receives it. I must check the direction before calling the light a witness." },
    { "Windows shine beyond the signal mast. The return marks point back into the circuit.\n\nSome of the lights can work without watchers. There may still be people behind the windows. I cannot substitute one uncertainty for another.", "The last cabinet has two interlocked faces. Opening either will close the other.\n\nI can interrupt the feed to expose the original, or complete it to witness the pen. The mechanism cannot show me both in one crossing.", "Her words and the keepers warning lie together. I must decide what i am willing to complete.\n\nThe original predates the locks; the live pen can reproduce a correction. These are testable facts. Whether I am rescuing her or doing her work is the conclusion I bring to the glass." },
};
